    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm looking at a specific scenario where the interruption wasn't a natural disaster, but more of a series of unpredictable and chaotic logistical failures at the port that stalled all incoming stock for months. Would a standard force majeure clause typically cover these kinds of chaotic, unpredictable disruptions, or is that considered a standard business risk?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Logistical failures at a port are highly fact-specific, and whether a standard clause covers them depends entirely on how \"acts of God,\" \"government intervention,\" or \"supply chain disruption\" are defined. If the contract doesn't specifically mention unforeseen global supply chain failures, most courts may view it as a standard business risk that the tenant is expected to mitigate. You should closely examine the clause to see if it encompasses systemic or governmental-level logistical paralysis.",
      "source": "target",
