      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Legally, courts typically require a concrete, identifiable event to qualify as force majeure, moving beyond mere inconvenience or \"chaos.\" While an argument for an \"irrational force\" might be persuasive in rhetoric, the contract needs a contractual trigger. You would need to argue that the *scale* and *unpredictability* reached a level beyond normal industry risk, essentially demonstrating an extraordinary external event, not just a difficult business cycle. Consult local Singaporean contract law precedent regarding supply chain volatility to support this argument.",
